From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anton Palgunov <toxblh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 23:44:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 79/99] v8: e2k: call C from a secondary stack, install JSPI

WebAssembly stack switching (JSPI, WasmFX) runs wasm code and the
switching builtins on secondary stacks, mmap'd apart from the main
stack. A C function called from there faulted: ccall lends the hardware
stack pointer %usd to C by moving it to sp - 64 with getsp, and from
another mapping that move goes off the data stack. %usd and the hardware
stacks cannot be switched from user mode (rwd to them is SIGILL), and
they need not be: the code of V8 keeps its frames in memory and runs in
one register window, only C makes hardware frames, and JS and runtime
functions called from wasm switch to the central stack anyway.

So ccall leaves %usd where enter() parked it when sp is not within
kStackReserve above it: the C frames then go under the parked pointer on
the main stack, where nothing is live. A C function takes its ninth and
further parameters at %usd + 64; CallC now gets the number of stack
words, and ccall copies them there (in place when %usd is at sp - 64).
switch_wasmfx_stack, with 9 parameters, needs that from a secondary
stack.

This reverts the guard of "v8: e2k: do not install JSPI": WebAssembly.
Suspending and WebAssembly.promising are installed again.

mjsunit/wasm stack-switching*, jspi*, wasmfx*, resume* pass in TurboFan
and Liftoff (18 of 18; before, 17 of them failed); all of mjsunit/wasm with
--stress-wasm-stack-switching fails only the known denormals and
speculative-inlining and the two wasm-to-JS wrapper profiler tests that
the upstream status file skips in that variant.
